1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,560
How many English words have you saved, but never used?

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:11,881
We look them up on the train. We write them in a
notebook at night. We tap save in a word app. The list

3
00:00:11,881 --> 00:00:14,950
becomes longer, but we almost never open it again.

4
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:23,393
The problem is not effort. A word is easier to
remember when we can recall the first scene, voice,

5
00:00:23,393 --> 00:00:28,850
and sentence around it. Without that context, it
becomes only a spelling on a list.

6
00:00:30,850 --> 00:00:37,825
Speaking has the same problem. A mistake happens in
today's meeting. We notice it, feel awkward for a

7
00:00:37,825 --> 00:00:41,900
second, and move on. Tomorrow, the same mistake comes
back.

8
00:00:43,900 --> 00:00:49,983
What if every word you meet, and every mistake you
make, could become tomorrow's practice? This is

9
00:00:49,983 --> 00:00:50,790
SpeakingLoop.

10
00:00:53,190 --> 00:00:57,380
When you watch an English video, click a useful word
in the subtitle.

11
00:00:57,880 --> 00:01:03,720
SpeakingLoop saves the word, the full sentence, the
source, and the moment where you found it.

12
00:01:05,220 --> 00:01:11,873
The next day, it brings you back to that scene. It
hides the key word and asks you to recall it. You do

13
00:01:11,873 --> 00:01:16,200
not just see the answer. You remember it, say it, and
use it again.

14
00:01:18,200 --> 00:01:21,060
Then, start a one-to-one speaking session.

15
00:01:21,560 --> 00:01:24,600
Yesterday I discuss the project with my manager.

16
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:31,770
Good try. Because it happened yesterday, say
discussed. Please try the full sentence again.

17
00:01:32,270 --> 00:01:35,640
Yesterday, I discussed the project with my manager.

18
00:01:36,140 --> 00:01:43,890
Great. Now try a stronger expression. Yesterday, I had
a useful discussion with my manager about the project.

19
00:01:44,390 --> 00:01:48,990
Yesterday, I had a useful discussion with my manager
about the project.

20
00:01:49,490 --> 00:01:53,030
Well done. How did that discussion change your plan?

21
00:01:53,830 --> 00:01:59,200
The loop continues: correct, repeat, improve, and move
to a new topic.

22
00:01:59,700 --> 00:02:06,417
At the end of the day, SpeakingLoop brings together
your words, grammar mistakes, and weak expressions. It

23
00:02:06,417 --> 00:02:10,600
creates a review for today and a clear practice plan
for tomorrow.

24
00:02:12,600 --> 00:02:19,864
Daytona gives our product a clean cloud sandbox. From
our public GitHub code, we can install, run, and test

25
00:02:19,864 --> 00:02:22,580
the full product in a fresh environment.

26
00:02:24,580 --> 00:02:31,554
Nosana provides GPU power for the after-class AI
review, turning each completed session into a personal

27
00:02:31,554 --> 00:02:37,850
plan. Daytona helps us build and run the experience.
Nosana helps us think after each lesson.

28
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:46,478
Do not let today's English disappear tomorrow. Turn
every word, every mistake, and every correction into

29
00:02:46,478 --> 00:02:48,390
your next better conversation.

30
00:02:50,690 --> 00:02:54,540
SpeakingLoop. Learn from your life. Speak better
tomorrow.
